| **q20** | **Delete the retired per-mnemonic encoder library (i73 L9), or keep it for the future i48c editor text→overlay encoder?** The v2 overlay (`insn_run.asm`) pre-folds every instruction, so the symbolic-path encoders were dead in production since `5e3e8eb`. | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-17): **delete per doctrine** (git history archives them for i48c; the release-gate byte-match makes it airtight since production pre-folds). Done as i73-L9 (PR #371): `intercepts.asm` cut to its lone test survivor `encode_ror_imm_word`; it + `sysname.asm` gated behind `BUILD_TESTS`; prod reclaims ~3.3 KB. Verified: prod+test builds + boot self-tests + release-gate byte-match. Follow-up i109 (vestigial soft-reject hook). | item registry i73 (L9), i109; `src/intercepts.asm`; `src/sysname.asm`; i48c |
| **q18** | **Brick 7 of the `http_main` port — build the hardware B-DOS HSAVE-per-record store leaf speculatively now, or leave it as the real-Trinity persist?** Bricks 1–6 (the full host-verifiable slice of the multi-file fetch loop) shipped (PRs #308–#314). Brick 7 migrates the real bootable `http_main` onto that loop + the real B-DOS HSAVE-per-record store leaf — non-host-verifiable (q16 gate) and footprint-sensitive (sha256 + buffers + a cap-sized staging buffer under `&10000`). Two snags: the record cap is a hardware RAM detail `span.go` refused to bake, and the HSAVE leaf can't be host-verified. (Full question in git history.) | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-17): **build Brick 7 speculatively** — *"if the earlier hardware phases pass we'd build it next anyway; worst case we adapt it, but it's probably still useful."* The build must assemble + fit under `&10000` + pass the host loop-tests, with the **record cap as a decoupled provisional `equ` constant** (pinned on hardware → q22) and the HSAVE store leaf honestly marked **unverified until Pete's Trinity test** (no faked success — CLAUDE.md §5). Work tracked under i100 (Brick 7); q22 carries the hardware cap-pin. | `docs/plans/z80-http-main-port-plan.md` (Brick 7); `tools/netboot-oracle/bdos/span.go`; `src/netboot/fw_span.asm`; `src/netboot/bdos_seam.asm`; item registry i100; q16, q22 |
| **q21** | **Rewrite the slow SHA-256 for the firmware-verify path, or accept the cost?** `sha256_compress` was ~730k T/block (memory-to-memory, subroutine-per-op); the i100/i101 deliverable streams a multi-MB firmware (`start.elf` ~3 MB) through it (`conn_verify`), so the on-SAM verify was ~hours. (Full original question in git history / PR #364.) | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-16): **do the register-oriented rewrite, keep cryptographic SHA-256** (firmware comes from a third-party CDN over plain HTTP → the SHA-256 pin is the integrity guarantee, not droppable). The bake-off confirmed no fast verified Z80 SHA-256 exists (oisee's "45k" is fabricated AI code that doesn't assemble/run/hash; field ~480–730k). Increment 1 landed as i102n — **−42.6%, the fastest verified Z80 SHA-256**. PR #365. | item registry i102m, i102n; `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; i100, i101 |
