| **q9** | **Full-fidelity source residency strategy (i59)** — the unstripped release comment corpus is 291,038 B (7,012 sidecar rows). MEASURED: real ZX0 at 4–8 KB blocks → ~83–92 KB resident, decode 36–71 ms/block (turbo); the Z80 greedy recompressor is byte-identical-proven at ~1.75 s per 4 KB dirty block. **Fits a 512 KB SAM with ~13+ pages spare; misses 256 KB by ~1 page** (→ lean on i40 spill for coldest blocks). Write-path decided: on-SAM greedy compressor (i60). Storage spill route: SAMDOS hooks → floppy (baseline tier) or B-DOS records (Trinity tier) — see `bdos-version-landscape.md`; SD select bytes now recovered (i61), so a raw SPI driver is likely unnecessary. Remaining sub-asks: (1) target machine 256 or 512 KB? (2) SR21 cover disk (carries SD article source + B-DOS 1.5t source) — recoverable from private materials? (3) Quazar programming manual recovery — now optional (nice-to-have confirmation). | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-10) — **the tool targets both 256 KB and 512 KB SAMs in principle, but the full unstripped release `.tbn` is allowed to exceed a 256 KB machine** (no over-optimising compression — the kernel grows over time anyway). A 256 KB SAM must still be able to *assemble*: provide a save-without-comments option (i65) so a 512 KB user can hand a stripped disk to a 256 KB user. Trinity page-persistence as a general technique: deferred, no value implementing now (i66). | i59, i60, i61, i62; `docs/notes/comment-compression-research.md`; `docs/notes/trinity-capabilities.md`; `docs/notes/bdos-version-landscape.md` |
| **q10** | **Boot-disk DOS migration** — swap the production/CI boot disks from SAMDOS 2 to B-DOS? Hook portability is proven (i62) and B-DOS is freeware-cleared (Edwin Blink's WoS grants), so the swap is licence-clean, and B-DOS is floppy-capable ("Improved SAMDOS"), keeping the no-Trinity tier first-class — though whether AL 1.5a boots cleanly with no Atom Lite attached is part of the i72 assessment. Options: (a) migrate disks + CI wholesale; (b) keep SAMDOS 2 as the shipped default and add a B-DOS disk-variant target now (lower risk, two paths to maintain); (c) defer the swap to Phase-3 onset when Trinity storage is actually consumed. i72 prepares the mechanics either way (the `-dos`/`-dos-name`/`-dos-load` flags on `tools/build-disk` now build a B-DOS boot disk without code changes); needs Pete's call on (a)/(b)/(c) + timing. **Evidence now in hand:** the hook surface is verified untouched in the Trinity fork (i71, static — all 39 hook vectors + handler bodies are 1.5a's bytes under relocation, only the sector-device layer swapped); B-DOS AL 1.5a boots and round-trips HSAVE/HGTHD/HLOAD under the i62 rig with the vendored binary; and 1.5t degrades gracefully with no Trinity attached (static observation — the SD layer is only reached on explicit record selection). | DONE — ✅ **RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-12)** — **incremental swap**. The `-dos` mechanics prep landed (i72, PR #187); execution is tracked as **i75**: prove the B-DOS-booted SimCoupé matrix green (incl. AL 1.5a behaviour with no Atom Lite attached — the untested corner), then flip the shipped/CI default to B-DOS while retaining samdos2 build capability. | i62, i71, i72, i75; `docs/notes/bdos-version-landscape.md` |
| **q11** | **Pi netboot mechanics + Colin Boot ROM specifics** (i84 §6.1/§6.3/§3.1) — all hardware/contact gated. (a) Does the Pi 400 boot-ROM EEPROM *static netboot* path (set `TFTP_IP` + `CLIENT_IP`/`SUBNET`/`GATEWAY`; `TFTP_PREFIX` selects the served subdir) work, so the SAM needs only a TFTP server and **no DHCP**? (b) What exact firmware file set + TFTP option set does the Pi's boot-ROM TFTP client request (note: on the Pi 4 the second-stage bootloader is in the Pi's own SPI EEPROM, so `bootcode.bin` is *not* served over TFTP — that is Pi 3 behaviour)? (c) What are the Trinity Boot ROM's autoboot affordances, and how is it obtained/configured (ask-Colin — Pete makes the contact)? | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-14) — (a) there is **no static-no-DHCP path** for the goal: the SAM **provides DHCP** (mandatory for Pi-3-class netboot, which has no static option; matches Pete's `dnsmasq` DHCP+TFTP setup) — tracked as new item **i86**; (b) the requested file list + option set is *execution research*, settled by **real packet capture** (Pete has Mac netboot logs) — and it is **model-general, not per-model SAM config**: the TFTP server serves by name from one flat store, so any aarch64 Pi is supported for free (Pi 3 + Pi 400 first); (c) **no Colin contact needed** — bootstrap is Pete's own **patched SAM ROM** (Trinity usable at power-on, no floppy; dump + reverse = new item **i87**), and the interface is fully covered by `trinity.mgt` + trinload + uIP + `reference/bdos`. Design doc revised accordingly. | `docs/specs/phase3-delivery-design.md` §3, §6; item registry i84, i83, i86, i87, i70 |
| **q12** | **Outbound delivery model** (i84 §6.4) — target **Model A (PXE pull**, SAM as TFTP server, bare-Pi boot — the actual goal) with **Model B (WRQ push** to a Linux Pi's `tftpd` — the old sketch's simpler model) as an optional early milestone, or skip B and go straight to A? | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-14) — **Model A only** (PXE pull, SAM = DHCP+TFTP server, bare-Pi boot — go straight for it). **Model B rejected** (WRQ push to a Linux Pi reintroduces a host dependency + a cooperating OS, contrary to the no-host-tools / self-hosting goal). | `docs/specs/phase3-delivery-design.md` §6.5; item registry i84, i83 |
| **q14** | **Live-test the i97 autonomous-loop monitor** (`tools/autonomous-loop/`) in Pete's real `screen` session. **PROVEN by live test 2026-06-15:** (a) a watcher-stuffed `/context` **readout reaches the agent's transcript** — ✅ (`screen -S … -p 0 -X stuff $'/context\r'` delivered the full readout into the running session) — **BUT delivery ≠ a model turn** (`/context` is a *local* command: it renders but does not invoke the model — see failure mode 3); (b) window-0 targeting — ✅ (claude in window 0, readout arrived); (c) the `SUBMIT` char — ✅ **`\r` confirmed** (it submitted; `\n` not needed). **Leg (d) — `/clear`+reprompt clean restart + the full cooperative loop:** ATTEMPTED 2026-06-15, **three failure modes found + fixed** (PR for i97 monitor robustness): (1) **a long stuffed line didn't submit** — the trailing `\r` of `stuff "<line>\r"` was absorbed into the paste burst, so the startup prompt sat in the input; fix: `submit()` sends a **separate, settled** Enter (`ALOOP_SUBMIT_SETTLE`, default 1s) as its own keystroke after each stuffed line. (2) **duplicate `/clear`** — Pete restarted the monitor without killing the old one, so **two instances** polled the same semaphores and each fired once (the agent saw `/clear` twice, the startup prompt arrived mangled/absent); fix: a **single-instance lock** (`$SEMA_DIR/monitor.lock`) makes a second monitor refuse to start. (3) **`/context` alone never resumed the agent (the ~1-item/hour stall)** — the task-done branch stuffed only `/context`, a *local* command that renders but does **not** invoke the model, so the agent idled after every item until the hourly hang-timeout nudge (a real prompt) kicked it; the 8-hour live log showed every `TASK_DONE -> /context` followed by ~60 min of silence then a `nudge` then the next item. Fix: the task-done branch now stuffs `/context` **then a short resume nudge** (`ALOOP_RESUME`, a real text line — *not* the startup prompt; the live agent keeps full context) so the next turn fires immediately. **Agent-side mitigation:** a `/clear` arriving mid-turn lands as a literal user message (not a command) and the agent must treat it as a **no-op** — recorded in the `feedback_autonomous_loop` memory; observed once this session (a double "/clear", caught + ignored, work unharmed). Re-run **unattended** (one instance, no human typing) to confirm leg (d) end-to-end. **✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-15):** the monitor is validated and working well in live use — Pete: *"the monitor is working perfectly … we can close it."* The three leg-(d) fixes (separate settled Enter; single-instance lock; `/context`+resume-nudge) hold. The one rough edge — the **drained-backlog ~30-min nudge token cost** (every cycle the monitor wakes an idle session that just says "nothing to do") — is **not a monitor defect** but an efficiency gap, tracked as new item **i103** (activity-gated quiescence: the agent writes a "leave me in peace" file on confirmed drain; the monitor stays silent until it detects new session activity, then auto-deletes the file and resumes — so it can't strand the loop). Monitor process **stopped 2026-06-15** (we returned to interactive mode); restart with `ALOOP_SESSION=<screen> tools/autonomous-loop/monitor.sh`. | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-15 — validated, working, closed; drain-nudge efficiency → i103) | item registry i97; `tools/autonomous-loop/README.md` |
| **q15** | **Firmware `.deb` extraction strategy** (i70/i88, surfaced by the 2026-06-15 mirror-check, design §7). The mirror-check proved the Pi netboot firmware is fetchable over **plain HTTP** from `archive.raspberrypi.org` (so on-SAM TLS / i88 is OPTIONAL), but the blobs arrive inside a `.deb` = an `ar` archive wrapping an **xz**-compressed `tar`. How should the SAM turn a fetched `.deb` into the served `./boot/*` files? Options: **(a)** on-SAM `.deb` handling — parse `ar` (trivial) + **xz-inflate** (LZMA2 — the hard part on a Z80, but a one-time/rare op) + untar; **(b)** point the i70 client at any HTTP server hosting the **already-extracted** boot files (host-tool-free for the end user; someone unpacks once) — simplest, no new Z80 code; **(c)** host-side staging (a PC unpacks + writes B-DOS records — the rejected host-tool path, but acceptable as an *optional* convenience). The agent leans **(b)** as the default (zero new Z80 burden, fits the "point-and-fetch" model) with **(a)** as the self-hosting purist upgrade. Non-blocking — the i70 wire path is done and works against any plain-HTTP source regardless; this only decides what that source serves. **SHARPENED 2026-06-15 (Pete's i100 requirement + his spectrum4 reference):** the downloader must let the user **pin an exact revision** (Pete pins git SHA `a43df3a…` — the last-good-before-broken, RPi firmware #1979) + **select a subset of files**. But Pete's reference fetches GitHub-raw-by-SHA over **HTTPS**, while the plain-HTTP apt archive is versioned by **apt package version, not git SHA** — so it can't serve an arbitrary pinned commit. So the source choice is now a 3-way fork: **(b1)** a **pre-extracted HTTP mirror keyed by revision** (`http://<host>/<rev>/boot/<file>` + a manifest for list-and-select) — plain HTTP, exact-revision-addressable, no `.deb`/xz, no TLS (**agent's lean**); **(b2)** the apt archive (coarser apt-version revisions + on-SAM `.deb`/xz extraction = option (a)); **(c2)** i88 TLS-to-GitHub for the exact SHA from the canonical source. **Awaiting Pete's source-model decision** (asked 2026-06-15). **FINDING 2026-06-15 (Pete asked: find an HTTP source addressable by git commit, else build i88):** a plain-HTTP, **commit-SHA-addressable** source **DOES exist** — **`http://cdn.githubraw.com/<owner>/<repo>/<40-char-sha>/<path>`** (a Cloudflare-fronted `raw.githubusercontent.com` proxy, Ryan Grove's rawgit lineage). Verified live: plain HTTP port 80, **zero redirects, no HSTS**, full-SHA path; the pinned-SHA `start.elf` (2.9 MB) came back **byte-identical** to canonical; whole boot tree resolves. So this **removes b1's need for a maintainer-hosted mirror** and means on-SAM TLS (i88) is **not strictly required**. **But** it's a **single free best-effort proxy** ("no uptime/support guarantees"; the lineage has died before — rawgit, gitcdn.link/.xyz, raw.fastgit.org, ghps.cc all dead) + **no Range support** (stream the whole file — fine, i99 does). Everything else (jsDelivr/statically/githack/SWH/git-dumb-HTTP/IPFS) is HTTPS-enforced or dead. **So the decision (Pete's, 2026-06-15):** **(a)** `cdn.githubraw.com` + a **SAM-side pinned-hash (SHA-256) verify** — self-service now, no TLS, but depends on a fragile proxy (**agent's lean**); **(b)** build **i88** (TLS→canonical GitHub) — durable but the hardest component; **(c)** both (proxy now, i88 the durable fallback). A SAM-side SHA-256 verify is worth building under *any* path (plain HTTP has no wire integrity; it's also an i88 building block). **✅ RESOLVED — Pete picked (c), 2026-06-15:** build **both**. **Active path:** fetch from `cdn.githubraw.com` (commit-SHA URL) + stream to storage (i99) + span records (q16) + a **SAM-side SHA-256 verify** against the pinned hash. **i88 (TLS→GitHub) is the durable fallback but deprioritized to the BOTTOM of the queue** — agents build it ONLY when nothing else is left to do. **First active increment: the SHA-256-on-Z80 primitive** (the verify now; an i88 building block later). **i88 scope-reduction (Pete):** since the firmware content is SHA-256-pinned anyway, i88 can be **github.com-only** (pin its key — no CA store) and may even **skip TLS cert verification** entirely (the content hash covers integrity), shrinking i88 to ~the TLS-1.3 crypto handshake + cipher (no X.509/cert-chain/CA complexity). | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-15: **c** — `cdn.githubraw.com` + SHA-256 verify active; i88 the durable fallback at **lowest priority**) | `docs/specs/phase3-delivery-design.md` §7; `~/git/spectrum4/src/spectrum4/firmware/Tupfile`; `http://cdn.githubraw.com`; item registry i70, i88, i100 |
| **q16** | **How does the netboot fetch write a multi-MB body to Trinity B-DOS storage incrementally?** (i99 design pass, 2026-06-15 — a genuine B-DOS-internals decision, surfaced by a real doc contradiction, not a manufactured blocker.) The streaming RAM-side (bounded buffer → abstract sink) is autonomous-eligible and proceeds regardless; this `qN` is only the **real-hardware binding** of that sink. The project's **SAMDOS-2-source audit** (`docs/notes/sam-stub-audit.md` bugs #1–#3) finds the byte-stream append hooks `HOFLE`/`HSBYT`/`HCFSM` **broken for external `RST 8` callers** (they assume `IX=dchan`, only set on the high-level HSAVE path), leaving whole-file `HSAVE` as the only *verified* write path — but HSAVE (a) needs the whole file contiguous in RAM (impossible for multi-MB) and (b) **can't encode >64 KB** in its UIFA length (i24). Yet `docs/notes/bdos-trinity-fork-analysis.md:41` asserts "HRECORD then ordinary HSAVE/HOFLE+HSBYT writes" as viable — an inference the i62 dual-run never exercised (it tested only HSAVE/HGTHD/HLOAD). **The two contradict; resolving needs Pete's off-repo B-DOS 1.5t analysis (`~/sam-archive/bdos/analysis/`) + his B-DOS knowledge.** Candidate bindings: **(a)** `HOFLE`+`HSBYT` incremental append (needs the 1.5t analysis to confirm B-DOS permits the external-caller path SAMDOS-2 breaks, maybe with an `IX=dchan` pre-setup); **(b)** raw-sector `HWSAD` streaming with the file header / sector-map / directory entry synthesised on the Z80 (powerful but re-implements B-DOS FS internals); **(c)** a convention of multiple **bounded** HSAVEs (each ≤64 KB, spanning records, reassembled at serve time) — the simplest, stays on the *verified* HSAVE hook, at the cost of a naming/spanning convention. Agent leans **(c)** as the lowest-risk default (verified hook only) pending Pete. **Non-blocking** — the host-verifiable streaming receive + abstract sink + tests proceed now; only the real flush is gated here. **✅ RESOLVED — Pete, 2026-06-15: option (c), multi-record/disk spanning via bounded `HSAVE`s.** Persist the firmware as a sequence of bounded files spanning multiple Trinity records/disks (each record ≈ one ~800 KB floppy image; a multi-MB blob spans several), each written with a plain `HSAVE` (the *verified* hook) and reassembled in order at serve time. This **sidesteps the append-hook contradiction entirely** — no `HOFLE`/`HSBYT` append to a growing file; just ordinary saves. **Also: the "(b) HSAVE can't encode >64 KB" premise above was WRONG** (Pete corrected it): the UIFA length is **pages-count(+34) × 16384 + len-mod-16K(+35..36)**, a 32-bit size (`bdos_difa_to_size` decodes it as 32-bit "because pages×16384 can exceed 16 bits"), so a single file can be **up to ~4 MB** (255 pages). The 64 KB was a conflation with the 16-bit *addressable window* (and with **i24**, which is the *assembler's* `OUT_LEN` output limit — unrelated to B-DOS file size). So the binding constraints are **RAM** (the HSAVE source can't hold multi-MB → stream, i99) + **per-record capacity** (~800 KB → span records), NOT a 64 KB file limit; the streaming window can be comfortably large (bounded by RAM + the record size). Remaining design detail (autonomous): the file-naming/ordering convention for the spanning records + the serve-time reassembly (the TFTP server reads chunks across the spanning files). | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-15 — option (c), multi-record spanning; the 64 KB premise corrected) | `docs/notes/sam-stub-audit.md`; `docs/notes/bdos-trinity-fork-analysis.md`; `docs/specs/samdos-file-io.md`; `src/netboot/bdos_seam.asm`; item registry i99, i24, i70 |
| **q17** | **Can the full on-SAM TLS 1.3 stack fit the bootable fetch's memory budget (the `&10000` ceiling), or does it need paging / a dedicated overlay?** (i88 handshake design pass, 2026-06-15 — surfaced while specing `docs/specs/tls13-handshake.md`; a genuine SAM-memory-architecture decision Pete owns, **not** a manufactured blocker.) The i88 cipher-suite primitives + AEAD are all built and host-verified as **standalone leaves** — deliberately **not** in the bootable image, so none of them has touched the `&10000` budget yet. A *real* TLS firmware-fetch, however, must carry the whole stack in the bootable binary at once: SHA-256 + HMAC + HKDF + Expand-Label + ChaCha20 + Poly1305 + X25519 + AEAD + the handshake code, **plus** their working buffers (X25519 ≈ 1 KB of field temporaries; AEAD ≈ 4 KB of PT/CT/mac-data; and the **server flight must be buffered to decrypt** — github's certificate chain alone is several KB). The bootable `netboot_http_boot.bin` already ends near **`&FA52`** (≈1.4 KB under `&10000`), so the combined TLS stack **will not** simply fit alongside it. Options: **(a)** page the TLS code/buffers in only for the one-time fetch (like the assembler's ENCTAB COMET trampoline) — the firmware fetch is rare, so a slow paged path is fine; **(b)** a dedicated TLS-fetch overlay binary loaded just for provisioning (separate from the daily netboot server), keeping the resident image lean; **(c)** stream-and-discard the server flight (don't buffer the cert chain — we don't validate it — only keep what the transcript hash + the parser need), shrinking the RAM peak. Likely a combination (c gives headroom; a or b gives the code room). **Blocking only the capstone (spec brick 6, the bootable integration); bricks 1–5 are host-verifiable standalone leaves that proceed regardless.** Agent leans (c)+(a). Secondary minor point (agent will default to **verify**, non-blocking): whether to verify the **server Finished** MAC given cert-validation is skipped. **Awaiting Pete** (he owns the SAM memory map). **✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-15):** Pete **delegates the SAM memory map / paging strategy entirely to the agent** — *"how you implement page swapping and what goes in what pages is entirely up to you … work out the most performant option."* So: **use paging.** Agent's plan: **(c)** stream-and-discard the server flight (no cert-chain buffering — cert validation is skipped) for RAM headroom **+ (a)** page the TLS code + working buffers in for the one-time fetch (ENCTAB-COMET-trampoline style; the firmware fetch is rare, so a slow paged path is fine). Server **Finished** MAC: **verified** (cheap, catches a broken handshake). Unblocks **i88 brick 6** (the bootable TLS integration); shrinking the combined footprint to ease the paging is tracked as **i102**. | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-15 — paging strategy delegated to the agent; use paging, agent owns the layout) | `docs/specs/tls13-handshake.md`; `docs/specs/phase3-delivery-design.md` §7; `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; `src/netboot/aead.asm`; item registry i88, i70 |

| **q19** | **Real-hardware entropy source for the TLS ephemeral X25519 scalar** (autonomous-loop, 2026-06-16; raised by the i88 brick-6 Z80 port plan). The TLS 1.3 client generates a per-handshake ephemeral X25519 key; the Z80 needs 32 random bytes for the scalar. The host test injects a fixed scalar (deterministic oracle), but real hardware needs a real source. **Low stakes:** the firmware content is independently SHA-256-pinned (i88 scope), so transport forward secrecy is not relied on for content integrity. **✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-16):** adopt a **build-time-injected per-build seed** as the default scalar source — at `make` time bake 32 bytes of host `/dev/urandom` into a generated (gitignored) `.asm` block; not in source, unique per build. Security analysis (agent, confirmed by Pete): a per-build *random, unpublished* scalar is **not** breakable by a passive network eavesdropper (ECDH hardness — unlike Konamiman's *known* `priv=1`), so it gives genuine confidentiality for **user-built** images. Caveats: (1) no forward secrecy (one reused scalar → a single leak is retroactive); (2) the seed lives in the disk artifact (extractable from the `.mgt`); (3) **distribution-model-dependent** — private only for user-built images; a *distributed* release `.mgt` shares one public-in-the-artifact seed (≈ a random constant → no confidentiality). **All moot for i88**, because security rests on the **SHA-256 content pin**, not the transport (the firmware is public + hash-verified) — even a fixed scalar would be "secure" for that purpose, and the per-build seed is strictly better than a source constant. **Upgrade path** (only if the TLS library is ever reused for real secrets / forward secrecy): keep the build seed as a per-device *salt* and mix in cheap per-handshake runtime entropy — the Z80 `R` register, the 50 Hz frame timer sampled at interrupt moments, ENC28J60 packet-arrival jitter — hashed through the already-built SHA-256 → the 32-byte scalar. The build seed is a good *ingredient*, not a good *sole source*. (The 6b/bootable wiring of this is i88 implementation work, not an open question.) | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-16 — build-time per-build seed default; the content-pin model makes it adequate; runtime-entropy mix is the documented upgrade path) | `docs/plans/z80-tls-client-port-plan.md` §Part 4 + Risks; `docs/specs/tls13-handshake.md` §Prior art + §Design decisions; item registry i88 |

| **q26** | **Netboot serve-manifest design — 4 sub-choices** (i114, design 2026-06-18). The manifest + storage-strategy design (`docs/specs/netboot-storage-manifest-design.md` §6) needed four buildable sub-choices: encoding; remote locator key; internal span-record naming; default storage strategy. | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-18): **(1) encoding = human-editable text**; **(2) remote locator key = both** (record number primary + optional name/label); **(3) internal span-record naming = hash-derived / content-addressed** — chosen over plain sequential so the §5 shared-files-by-reference dedup is automatic; **(4) default storage strategy = highest-free** (keeps the user's low slots for their own disks). Locked into `netboot-storage-manifest-design.md` §6; i114's host-verifiable slice is now buildable. | `docs/specs/netboot-storage-manifest-design.md` §6; item registry i114; i93, i95, i70 |
| **q27** | **i119 free-record detection/selection policy.** Originally posed (2026-06-19, the B2 emulation work) as a fork between a faithful HRECORD-probe + per-record stamp/label inspect vs a brittle B-DOS-internal `nmi.sp` error trap, on the mistaken finding that the central record list is *not* user-reachable. | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-19). The premise was wrong: the central record list **is** reachable (the `RECORD` BASIC command lists every record by name — `bdos15a.src.txt:886-906`; the only narrow truth is there is no *dedicated* RST-8 "list records" hook). So detection = **read the record list directly** against the **frozen on-card layout** (read list sectors `1..base-1`, parse the 16-byte entries, free ⇔ masked first name byte == 0 — `frec3x`, `bdos15a.src.txt:946-948`), depending only on frozen interfaces (port map + SD protocol + on-card format, identical 1.5a↔1.5t per i71) and **zero** B-DOS internal addresses — **NOT** the coarse HRECORD-probe / `nmi.sp` trap the question posed (that fork only existed because of the mistaken "not reachable" finding). UX = list / manual-pick / auto-pick of a free record + **show-name + confirm before overwrite** (do both); the confirm gate is mandatory (Trinity SD is a shared user resource). Design: `docs/specs/trinity-record-detection-design.md`. | `docs/specs/trinity-record-detection-design.md`; `src/netboot/bdos_seam.asm` (`bdos_inspect_record`); `memory/trinity_storage_shared_resource`; `~/sam-archive/bdos/analysis/bdos15a.src.txt`; item registry i119, i114, i121, i122 |
| **q28** | i115f reshape sequencing — markdown-first vs validated-YAML-at-migration (2026-06-19, registry model revision). | DONE — ✅ RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-19): option **(b)** — the audited reshape lands directly in **validated YAML** via the bounded-batch single-PR migration (rename live registries to `.old`; migrate ~15-20 items/batch via the CLI with ids preserved, split per the atomic-item rule, `BLOCKED`→`depends_on` edges, over-bound detail relocated to a named home; delete-as-you-go from `.old`; per-batch local CI + independent info-loss review; closed questions retire). Markdown-first (a) rejected: the 4-column table can't express the new `depends_on`/transient-question model and reintroduces the unvalidated hand-editing the effort exists to kill. See the plan's "Migration execution process". | `docs/plans/registry-structured-source.md` ("Migration execution process"); `docs/specs/registry-source-of-truth-design.md`; item registry i115, i115f, i115d |

